#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151943 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152155 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "39d79985-57fa-554a-b697-4bd6dd71cbe2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Basis of the Column Space of a Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "fc21ef44-25b7-5157-bbdb-c1284d1aa7ae" :question/math-academy-id "e-6254" :question/problem "Consider the matrix ![](images/81/81c99a47d72cc5e259acc8b4b33bf58c71c5d20e6c695beeeb71e0c784b614d5.png) Find a basis $\\mathcal B$ of $\\textrm{Col}(A).$" :question/worked-solution "In order to find a basis of $\\textrm{Col}(A),$ we need reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 8\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to linearly independent vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the $1$st and $2$nd columns are pivot columns, while the $3$rd column is a non-pivot column.\n\nRemember that we need to pick the pivot columns from the *original matrix*, not from the reduced one. \n\nThe $1$st and $2$nd columns from the original matrix are $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2.$ So, a basis of $\\textrm{Col}(A)$ is $\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 \\},$ that is to say, \n\n![](images/02/02a95bd3513fcd70ea74936f330472e5bf5a1e14b189da9c30c5876782d12632.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151944 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152156 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "db27967e-f80c-587b-9b10-28f1a5518f57" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Given Set Is a Basis of the Column Space of a Matrix With Two Rows"}] :question/id #uuid "302059c1-d807-54d2-badc-a4a19b2f9c05" :question/math-academy-id "e-6255" :question/problem "The matrix $A$ is given below. Let $\\mathbf{a}_1$, $\\mathbf{a}_2$, and $\\mathbf{a}_3$ be the respective columns of $A.$ Determine if ![](images/f7/f7e27a29b580edc016bf479e983cb17c5e61ebb800b03e671b89b5d556056533.png) is a basis of $\\textrm{Col}(A).$ \nIf that is the case, find $x_1 \\cdot x_2$, where $\\mathbf{a}_3=x_1\\mathbf{a}_1+x_2\\mathbf{a}_2.$\n\n![](images/cc/cce279327716181e5b5c5c1e95e7eac34c2b93a78a1f7e773393ffbf608cb1f4.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, we reduce the matrix $A$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 9 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 17\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 9 \\\\ 0 & -5 & -10\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=(-\\frac{1}{5}){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd columns. So, a basis of $\\textrm{Col}(A)$ is $\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2 \\},$ that is to say,\n\n![](images/f0/f083a27cf3de0a6150020b6f5887096258880d4f5e30de1cfb3bb091b79a16f9.png)\n\nNow, since the $3$rd column of the reduced matrix is a non-pivot column, then the entries in that column tell us the coefficients needed to express $\\mathbf{a}_3$ as a linear combination of all the other columns in the original matrix. So, we have\n\n![](images/96/96f39576ea8196a88248035262eb7cd006c134717f695b23c575667b6562f6e0.png)\n\nThis gives us $x_1 \\cdot x_2 = 5 \\cdot 2=10.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151945 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152157 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c3bf9ea3-d57f-53f6-aa3c-eddffe1a8cfa" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Given Set Is a Basis of the Column Space of a Matrix For a Larger Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "d5bcbec9-0b72-5ba1-ad84-c770cf3a0c0b" :question/math-academy-id "e-6272" :question/problem "The matrix $A$ is given below. Let $\\mathbf{a}_1$, $\\mathbf{a}_2$,  $\\mathbf{a}_3$, and $\\mathbf{a}_4$ be the respective columns of $A.$ Determine if ![](images/f7/f7e27a29b580edc016bf479e983cb17c5e61ebb800b03e671b89b5d556056533.png) is a basis of $\\textrm{Col}(A).$ If that is the case, find $x_1 \\cdot x_2$, where $\\mathbf{a}_4=x_1\\mathbf{a}_1+x_2\\mathbf{a}_2.$\n\n![](images/70/70f59cf06f15bb416d32ffa9b28d604f4a842273ac8bd04d9f59b121ca1dc40d.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, we reduce the matrix $A$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & -4 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -6 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -14 & -4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -6 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -14 & -4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -6 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -12 & -6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -6 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd columns. So, a basis of $\\textrm{Col}(A)$ is $\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2 \\},$ that is to say,\n![](images/9c/9c6ae7fc8b0c044bf2286ea2b9d41c7a427deebf8184a3515ad2b20edc682afc.png)\n\nNow, since the $4$th column of the reduced matrix is a non-pivot column, then the entries in that column tell us the coefficients needed to express $\\mathbf{a}_4$ as a linear combination of all the other columns in the original matrix. So, we have\n\n![](images/4a/4a0d70f028211fafeab4353e5c5e34cb1895be2350f5fd0211b6e76856dea3e0.png)\n\nThis gives us $x_1 \\cdot x_2 = 2 \\cdot (-3) = -6.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247221 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152156 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "db27967e-f80c-587b-9b10-28f1a5518f57" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Given Set Is a Basis of the Column Space of a Matrix With Two Rows"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1640f98f-b934-544d-b764-303053864f27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244517} {:answer/id #uuid "1f7e1df3-4580-5975-b457-6c0295c1f461" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244591} {:answer/id #uuid "53145b12-05ac-544a-99da-924b20f9b963" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245047} {:answer/id #uuid "5891d6a5-5899-58e5-aad9-238b06855faa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\text{{}{d}_{1},{d}_{3}\\text{}}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Col}(D)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245097} {:answer/id #uuid "ecfbaa5c-8266-5fb2-9b94-fdf08e2337ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246409}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ecfbaa5c-8266-5fb2-9b94-fdf08e2337ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246409} :answer-field/id #uuid "18d39719-9109-5df8-9dbf-ca0c2b5cad6d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246589}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1560ce17-f00a-5fcc-81e8-d48fb2971bfa" :question/math-academy-id "q-111468" :question/problem "The matrix $D$ is given below. Let ${d}_{1}$, ${d}_{2}$, and ${d}_{3}$ be the respective columns of $D.$ Determine if $B=\\text{{}{d}_{1},{d}_{3}\\text{}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(D).$ \nIf that is the case, find ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{3}$, where ${d}_{2}={x}_{1}{d}_{1}+{x}_{3}{d}_{3}.$ \n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -6 & 2 \\\\ -4 & 24 & 8\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we reduce the matrix $A$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -6 & 2 \\\\ -4 & 24 & 8\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -6 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 16\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{16}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -6 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $3$rd columns. So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(D)$ is ${{d}_{1},{d}_{3}},$ that is to say,\n\n$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the $2$nd column of the reduced matrix is a non-pivot column, then the entries in that column tell us the coefficients needed to express ${d}_{2}$ as a linear combination of all the other columns in the original matrix. So, we have\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 24\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{d}_{2}}={{(-6)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{1}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{d}_{1}}+{{(0)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{3}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{d}_{3}}.\n$$\n\nThis gives us ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{3}=-6\\cdot 0=0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152579 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152156 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "db27967e-f80c-587b-9b10-28f1a5518f57" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Given Set Is a Basis of the Column Space of a Matrix With Two Rows"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a5750330-79d7-50e2-9ded-15a59101eba5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149982} {:answer/id #uuid "b785a5e7-ecc7-5455-a788-080e518def4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149983} {:answer/id #uuid "c9377f2f-9488-5514-aeeb-48e23c16df47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149984} {:answer/id #uuid "2d828a63-df06-503d-8a71-fa2db4041f11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${a_{1}, a_{2}}$ is not a basis of $Col(A)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149985} {:answer/id #uuid "6fe9633c-3b72-5df8-adc7-1006f8331422" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149986}] :answer-field/id #uuid "60a2d415-9e9a-5c72-aafa-7f2b41592aa6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149981}] :question/id #uuid "c555c8c9-de13-50dd-99c1-9c9975c94651" :question/math-academy-id "q-34462" :question/problem "The matrix $A$ is given below. Let $a_{1}$, $a_{2}$, and $a_{3}$ be the respective columns of $A$. Determine if $B = {a_{1}, a_{2}}$ is a basis of $Col(A)$. If that is the case, find $x_{1} \\cdot x_{2}$, where $a_{3} = x_{1}a_{1} + x_{2}a_{2}$.\n\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -6\\end{bmatrix}$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318982 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152155 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "39d79985-57fa-554a-b697-4bd6dd71cbe2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Basis of the Column Space of a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18a83efb-bbdc-5f60-a0dd-e2432fc1e2e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317641} {:answer/id #uuid "3a062778-5280-55f7-9b55-8a725709712f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317753} {:answer/id #uuid "8c3efe41-2012-53b2-82f8-f9b3ac02c754" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318039} {:answer/id #uuid "9872000f-8488-50b1-a7e7-ab89516f120b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318087} {:answer/id #uuid "bba2e88e-6bfc-57f0-810e-104a7b8ad74d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318188}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "18a83efb-bbdc-5f60-a0dd-e2432fc1e2e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317641} :answer-field/id #uuid "eb5ef360-4ae2-56e4-8b56-8bb54c7ef0fd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318917}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "28419572-5f5f-5d91-aa00-334d8b1c45ff" :question/math-academy-id "q-34514" :question/problem "Given the matrix \n$F=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 \\\\ 7 & -2\\end{aligned}].$ Find a basis $B$ of $\\text{Col}(F).$" :question/worked-solution "In order to find a basis of $\\text{Col}(F),$ we reduce the matrix $F$ to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 \\\\ 7 & -2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+7{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 19\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to linearly independent vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the $1$st and $2$nd columns are pivot columns.\n\nRemember that we need to pick the pivot columns from the *original matrix*, not from the reduced one. \n\nThe $1$st and $2$rd columns from the original matrix are ${f}_{1}$ and ${f}_{2}.$ So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(F)$ is ${{f}_{1},{f}_{2}},$ that is to say, \n\n$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345151 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152157 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c3bf9ea3-d57f-53f6-aa3c-eddffe1a8cfa" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Given Set Is a Basis of the Column Space of a Matrix For a Larger Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1544e852-2362-55d4-99ce-5e8f32d8266c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${{v}_{1},{v}_{3}}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Col}(V)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342425} {:answer/id #uuid "3214ac66-089e-5f1a-b5f5-27127d89af81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342662} {:answer/id #uuid "5e6f8a38-0a5a-5b8d-9bbd-8d6b55a7767b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343032} {:answer/id #uuid "69df0ecf-34a7-5ab2-a85a-47fd55e5f70c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343142} {:answer/id #uuid "fd9fef81-c774-530d-bce3-3ec15ebb79e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344469}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1544e852-2362-55d4-99ce-5e8f32d8266c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${{v}_{1},{v}_{3}}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Col}(V)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342425} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab78a2e2-73c6-516c-bb41-33e676435ed4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344794}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c466a2ff-d594-54f8-8655-5aeb347fd10d" :question/math-academy-id "q-75980" :question/problem "The matrix $V$ is given below. Let ${v}_{1}$, ${v}_{2}$,  ${v}_{3}$ and ${v}_{4}$ be the respective columns of $V.$\nDetermine if $B=\\text{{}{v}_{1},{v}_{3}\\text{}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(V).$ \nIf that is the case, find ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{3}$, where ${v}_{2}={x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}.$ \n\n$$\nV=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 & -6 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 10 & 20 & 10 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 4 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we reduce the matrix $V$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 & -6 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 10 & 20 & 10 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 4 & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=(-\\frac{1}{3}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 10 & 20 & 10 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 4 & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 10 & 20 & 10 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{10}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st, $2$nd, and $4$th columns. So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(V)$ is ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{4}}.$ \n\nTherefore, we conclude that $B={{v}_{1},{v}_{3}}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Col}(V).$"}]}
